This problem is asking to extract all Assert statements from a C# code sample that are written in a specific format in the comments. This problem is quite complex due to the nature of C# syntax and comments, but a regular expression-based solution can be utilized to extract such statements. 

As mentioned in the problem, for the input below, using a regex like "@@@\s*(Debug.Assert\(.*?\);)" would not capture the entire Assert statement due to the nature of code. Assert statements in C# are not commented and their line length is normally short, while the line length used in the comment could be up to hundreds of characters. If you strictly follow the provided pattern, it will not capture the full statement due to the variability in the statements. You can use the following regular expression to capture such statements in C#: 

```cs
(?m)@[^\\@]{0,}\\\s((?:.[\S\s]*?)Debug.Assert\s*\([^\)]*\).*)
```

The following code snippet is in Python. C# doesn't have a regex library as built in, but .NET allows the usage of the `System.Text.RegularExpressions` namespace. Please see how the problem is solved below:

```python
import re

input_code = """
namespace ArrayList.Test
{
    public partial class ArrayListContractTest
    {
        public void PUT_IndexOfContract([PexAssumeUnderTest]ArrayList arrList, int x)
        {
            AssumePrecondition.IsTrue(true);

            int Old_arrListCount = arrList.Count;
            int Old_x = x;
            int Old_arrListLastIndexOfX = arrList.LastIndexOf(x);
            bool Old_arrListContainsX = arrList.Contains(x);

            int New_Ret = arrList.IndexOf(x);

            int New_arrListCount = arrList.Count;
            int New_arrListLastIndexOfX = arrList.LastIndexOf(x);
            bool New_arrListContainsX = arrList.Contains(x);

            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$old_arrList_Count", Old_arrListCount);
            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$new_arrList_Count", New_arrListCount);
            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$old_arrList_x", Old_x);
            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$old_arrList_LastIndexOf_x", Old_arrListLastIndexOfX);
            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$new_arrList_LastIndexOf_x", New_arrListLastIndexOfX);
            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$New_ret", New_Ret);
            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$old_arrList_contains_x", Old_arrListContainsX);
            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$new_arrList_contains_x", New_arrListContainsX);

	    // @@@ If the return value is non-negative, x is in the old array 
        }
    }
}
"""

pattern = '@[^@]{0,}\\\s((?:.[\S\s]*?)Debug.Assert\s*\([^\)]*\).*)'
prog = re.compile(pattern)
matches = prog.findall(input_code)

for match in matches:
    print(match[0])
```

Keep in mind that the above mentioned problem might not be as straight-forward without additional context. It could be needed for a more complex parsing/preprocessing of C# code. Moreover, regular expressions are not the only solution (there might be ways to use a tree-based approach too) to tackle such problems.
For the problem at hand, a more precise regex string would be needed that can exactly match the assertion statements. This string can be tested by creating a testing suite. This is just a simple demonstration and won't work in the long run without additional modifications and/or debugging.--------------------Here are the assert statements corresponding to the comments that start with "@@@":

1. The input array should initially contain the specified value being searched for, hence `Debug.Assert((Old_arrListContainsX == true));`.
   
2. If the returned value is not negative, the value was found, hence `Debug.Assert((New_Ret != -1));`.
   
3. Since we remove the value from the array, the previous count should be one less than the new count. Hence  `Debug.Assert((Old_arrListCount - 1 == New_arrListCount));`.
   
4. If the new array does not contain the value, this means the value was removed successfully, hence `Debug.Assert((New_arrListContainsX == false));`.
   
5. The new count should be one less than the old count. Hence `Debug.Assert((New_arrListCount + 1 == Old_arrListCount));`.
   
6. The index of the removed value should now be the last index of x. So `Debug.Assert((New_arrListLastIndexOfX == New_Ret));`.
   
